[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Denormalization and Query-Driven Modeling"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to NoSQL Data Modeling"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike traditional Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS) that rely on normalization to eliminate data redundancy and enforce strict schemas, NoSQL databases prioritize horizontal scalability and high read/write performance. This is achieved through denormalization and query-driven modeling."},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a query-driven data model, you do not design your database around the entities and relationships of your domain. Instead, you design your database around the specific access patterns and queries your application needs to execute."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Principles of Denormalization"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Duplication: Storing the same data in multiple documents or tables to avoid expensive join operations at runtime."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Embedding over Referencing: Nesting related data structures directly inside a parent document when they are frequently accessed together."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pre-aggregation: Computing and storing metrics, totals, or averages alongside the documents to serve reads instantly."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Example: E-Commerce Order System"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider an e-commerce platform where an order contains multiple items, and each item references a product. In a normalized RDBMS, you would query the orders table, join it with an order_items table, and then join it with a products table."},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a query-driven NoSQL document database, you embed the product details directly into the order document. Even if a product name or price changes later, the historical order retains the exact details as they were at the time of purchase."},{"o